{"type": "assistant", "message": {"model": "claude-opus-5-5", "id": "msg_vrtx_011Cfzy9FMHqnm2SnNK5jC8i", "type": "message", "role": "assistant", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I recovered all 11 records and wrote them to `/app/recovered.json`, sorted by id.\n\n**The problem:** the WAL file had been scrambled by XORing every byte with `0x42`. I spotted it from the file header: its first 4 bytes, XORed with `0x42424242`, give the standard WAL magic number `0x377f0682`. Flipping every byte back produced a valid WAL, and SQLite then read the extra records from it.\n\n**Recovered data:**\n\n| id | name | value |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | apple | 150 |\n| 2 | banana | 250 |\n| 3 | cherry | 300 |\n| 4 | date | 400 |\n| 5 | elderberry | 500 |\n| 6 | fig | 600 |\n| 7 | grape | 700 |\n| 8 | honeydew | 800 |\n| 9 | kiwi | 900 |\n| 10 | lemon | 1000 |\n| 11 | mango | 1100 |\n\n**Change to the database files:** when my script closed the connection, SQLite merged the WAL changes into `main.db` and deleted `main.db-wal`. So `/app/main.db` now holds all 11 records by itself, and there's no WAL file left in `/app`. Copies of both original files are inside the container at `/tmp/main.db.bak` and `/tmp/wal.bak`."}], "container": null, "stop_reason": null, "stop_sequence": null, "stop_details": null, "usage": {"input_tokens": 2, "cache_creation_input_tokens": 1101, "cache_read_input_tokens": 17407, "cache_creation": {"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens": 1101, "ephemeral_1h_input_tokens": 0}, "output_tokens": 8}, "input_transformations": [], "context_management": null}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "9255b172-f49d-426e-bf89-7d64c4a98801", "uuid": "27efd5a6-9b9b-4156-9750-f02da1b71225", "timestamp": "2026-10-13T21:45:36.676Z", "request_id": "req_vrtx_011Cfzy9FKog1Nr21fxEEuTf"}
